# Canonical copy of /etc/udev/rules.d/99-ratzek-ssd.rules on ratzek (the Pi).
# Deployed BY HAND: scp here -> /etc/udev/rules.d/, then `udevadm control --reload &&
# udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=scsi --action=change`.
#
# Root lives on a HIKVISION ESSD-T200N behind a VIA Labs VL716 bridge (usb 2109:0716) driven by
# uas. That bridge wedges: userspace stopped writing on 2026-06-30 and again on 2026-08-30 (45h),
# while the kernel kept forwarding packets.
#
# Bound how long the SCSI error handler waits on a command before giving up, instead of the 30s
# default. When the bridge stops answering it is the recovery path itself that hangs, and every
# second here is a second the whole rootfs is frozen.
# Rollback: echo 30 > /sys/block/sda/device/timeout
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="scsi", KERNEL=="0:0:0:0", ATTR{timeout}="15"

# NOT SET, and this is a finding rather than an omission — both were tried on the live host and
# rejected by the kernel:
#
#   ATTR{queue_depth}="1"     -> /sys/block/sda/device/queue_depth is mode 0444 under uas; udev
#                                logs "Failed to write ATTR{...}/queue_depth: Permission denied".
#                                The uas driver fixes the depth; it is not tunable at runtime.
#   ATTR{eh_deadline}="20"    -> write to /sys/class/scsi_host/host0/eh_deadline is refused for
#                                this transport as well.
#
# Consequence for the recovery plan: there is NO reversible way to disable UAS command queueing on
# this host. The only lever that turns it off is usb-storage.quirks=2109:0716:u in cmdline.txt,
# which drops the device to bulk-only transport — a boot-path edit on a site with no physical
# access and no remote power cycle. Any plan that treats "try queue_depth=1 first" as the cheap
# alternative to that edit is working from an assumption this hardware does not support.
